\begin{center}
{
\Large
Shell Topic 07: sed
}
\vspace{0.1in}
\end{center}

\begin{note}
The \lstinline{sed} (\emph{stream editor}) edits files line-by-line.
The most common \lstinline{sed} command is \emph{substitution} \lstinline{s/REGEX/REPLACEMENT/FLAGS}.
By default, substitution replaces only the first match on each line.
The \lstinline{g} (global) flag replaces every match on each line.
The \lstinline{/} in \lstinline{s/a/b/} is not special.
Sed uses whatever character immediately follows the \lstinline{s} as the delimiter.
Therefore \lstinline{s;a;b;}, \lstinline{s|a|b|}, and \lstinline{s,a,b,} are all equivalent to \lstinline{s/a/b/}.
\end{note}
